    <text>
        <front>
            <div type="summary">
                <p>
                    In this letter to Dorothy Lange, Edward Upward inquires whether the Book Society already possess a photograph of him. <lb/>
                    Furthermore, he requests for plans to publish his book in America, in the case that arrangements have not yet been made.
                </p>
            </div>
        </front>
        <body>
            <div type="letter">
                <opener>
                    <dateline>
                        <date when="1938-02-13">
                            Feb 13th 1938 <note><p>Recipient's address is missing in the original letter.</p></note> 
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                   
                    <salute>
                        Dear Miss Lange, <lb/>
                    </salute>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    Many thanks for your letter. <lb/>
                    
                    I enclose a few biographical details for <lb/>
                    
                    the <title>Book Society News</title>. I have not <unclear reason="illegible" cert="high">a good</unclear> photograph <lb/>
                    
                    of myself in my possession, but <persName key="Q5919346">Howard <lb/>
                    
                    Coster</persName> has recently done a very good one. I <lb/>
                    
                    don’t know whether there is any possibility <lb/>
                    
                    that <del rend="strikethrough">be prepared</del> the <orgName key="BSOC">Book Society</orgName> might <lb/>
                    
                    be prepared to buy one from him: if not, <lb/>
                    
                    please let me know and I will obtain one. <lb/>
                    
                    I have just received from <persName key="MAPP0034">John J. <lb/>
                    
                    Trounstine</persName> of <placeName key="Q60">New York</placeName> an inquiry as <lb/>
                    
                    to whether any arrangements have been <lb/>
                    
                    made to publishing my book in <placeName key="Q30">America</placeName><note><p>With Upward's reference to New York, there is a high possibility, that he means the USA</p></note>. <lb/>
                    
                    If no arrangements have been made, would <pb/>
                    
                    there be any objection to permitting him <lb/>
                    
                    to look for a publisher there? <lb/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <salute>
                        Yours sincerely, <lb/>
                    </salute>
                    <signed>Edward Upward.</signed>
                    <adress>
                        <addrLine>39, Woodleigh Gardens,</addrLine>
                        <addrLine>s. w. 16</addrLine> <note><p>This is the sender's address provided at the top of the original letter, moved intentionally to the closer to avoid confusion.</p></note>
                    </adress>
                </closer>
            </div>
